WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.169
Okay, so I try to recycle.

00:00:02.169 --> 00:00:03.790
I’ve got my grocery tote bag.

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:05.620
I even have solar panels on my roof.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:08.330
But in the back of my mind, I can’t help
thinking:

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:18.620
Does any of this actually make a difference
when it comes to climate change?

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:23.660
If you read the headlines, you quickly begin
to see that climate change is a massive problem.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:27.750
So is my reusable bag really going to change
the world?

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.850
But not everyone feels that way.

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:34.060
This is all of my trash from the past four
years…

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.850
Oh, my god.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:37.540
This is Lauren Singer.

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:42.340
She runs a website where she gives tips and
answers questions about living a zero-waste

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:43.340
life.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.730
Okay, so you’ve got tiny little ends and
bits and things…

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:46.730
Yeah.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:50.530
Are you really telling me that everything
else that you use for four years—

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:51.530
Is—

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:53.680
You’ve found some other use for?

00:00:53.680 --> 00:01:00.230
Totally, is compostable, infinitely reusable,
or 100% easily recyclable.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:04.920
You may look at the extremely eco-friendly
way Lauren is living and find it inspiring.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.070
Or maybe, like me, you’re totally skeptical.

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:10.670
But a lot of what she’s doing is actually
pretty simple.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.979
When she wants coffee, she brings her own
cup.

00:01:12.979 --> 00:01:18.340
Or let’s say she wants to buy a pastry;
she’ll put it into a reusable cotton bag.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:21.859
A safety razor instead of plastic ones.

00:01:21.859 --> 00:01:26.670
There’s all this disposable stuff in our
lives that we’re not even thinking about.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:30.829
And what Lauren’s done is find some easy
substitutes.

00:01:30.829 --> 00:01:33.889
Everything else ends up in the jar.

00:01:33.889 --> 00:01:37.450
This is macaroni-and-cheese packaging, and
this was, like, four years ago, right when

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:38.450
I started.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.869
That was, that was my weekend at Dad’s house.

00:01:40.869 --> 00:01:41.869
So these are…

00:01:41.869 --> 00:01:42.869
Oh, I know what these are.

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:43.869
Plastic straws…

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:44.869
Hot chocolate.

00:01:44.869 --> 00:01:46.039
This was a bad day, wasn’t it, for you?

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:50.259
No, actually someone sent that to me in the
mail.

00:01:50.259 --> 00:01:52.549
These aren’t huge trash problems.

00:01:52.549 --> 00:01:55.789
The EPA isn’t up in arms about plastic straws.

00:01:55.789 --> 00:02:00.020
But you can see how these little bits of waste
can really add up.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:04.340
The United States is the No. 1 trash-producing
country in the world.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:09.409
If every country lived like the US, we’d
need over four Earths to make all the stuff

00:02:09.409 --> 00:02:10.409
we consume.

00:02:10.409 --> 00:02:12.909
Do you think little things make a big difference?

00:02:12.909 --> 00:02:13.909
Totally.

00:02:13.909 --> 00:02:19.530
If you reduce single-use coffee cups from
your routine and you’re a daily coffee drinker,

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.030
that’s 365 cups per year.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.409
That’s not an insignificant change.

00:02:24.409 --> 00:02:26.049
If every single person did that,

00:02:26.049 --> 00:02:29.379
that’s a massive shift toward a more sustainable
future.

00:02:29.379 --> 00:02:32.590
And good policy can encourage this kind of
shift.

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:33.770
Take plastic bags.

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:37.349
Americans throw away about 100 billion a year.

00:02:37.349 --> 00:02:39.770
But California is trying to change this.

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:44.770
Three communities have found that if you offer
a plastic bag for free, 75 percent of people

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:45.770
will take it.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:50.680
But if you charge 10 cents for a bag, only
16 percent take it.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:56.159
It’s subtle, but this small fee makes people
question whether they really need a bag.

00:02:56.159 --> 00:02:59.170
And it reminds people to bring their own.

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:00.830
Communities across the country are beginning
to

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:01.980
adopt this policy,

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.599
and it could create a large-scale shift.

00:03:04.599 --> 00:03:10.769
If New York City had a bag fee, we could save
roughly 7 billion plastic bags a year.

00:03:10.769 --> 00:03:14.750
And without good policy, it can be really
hard to do the right thing.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:19.400
Take recycling: In a place like Missoula,
Montana, where I live, you can’t recycle

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.879
glass because doing so, it turns out, costs
my city too much.

00:03:22.879 --> 00:03:29.090
I think this is a fundamental flaw of governments
and their relationship with businesses.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:32.980
Businesses aren’t held accountable for products
that they’re putting into the waste stream.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:38.079
So they’re allowed to sell glass in Montana,
where there’s no adequate recycling,

00:03:38.079 --> 00:03:40.140
and completely wipe their hands free

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:44.870
and not have to subsidize any infrastructure
to adequately recycle their product.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:50.390
So that responsibility for disposing of that
product falls on you, as a resident and the

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:51.390
government.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:52.870
That is completely unfair.

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:57.239
The funny thing is, we used to have a really
great system for dealing with glass.

00:03:57.239 --> 00:04:00.730
After you were done with a bottle, you would
just return it.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:03.230
Companies would clean it and use it again
and again.

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:09.989
Around the 1950s, companies began experimenting
with single-use bottles and cans.

00:04:09.989 --> 00:04:12.900
Lots of other things became single use too.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:16.530
Like Don Draper here, people were just tossing
their garbage wherever.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:19.020
And all this trash started to annoy people.

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:22.000
Do you remember that very famous commercial?

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Of this Native American,

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.890
he’s, like, going down a river and there’s
all this waste, and a tear goes down.

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:31.840
People start pollution; people can stop it.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:36.030
And it’s often credited for quote unquote
“cleaning up America”

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:38.720
because we were reminded that we need to pick
up our trash.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:43.470
You see this commercial every Earth Day, but
it was actually funded by a group of companies

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:46.850
— many of them from the can and bottle industry.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:51.610
They were worried that states would ban their
single-use products because people were getting

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:53.050
sick of all the trash.

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:57.560
So they created this incredible ad, which
is very powerful, which made us pick up trash.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:04:59.510
Which is actually trash that they were creating…

00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:00.510
And selling to us…

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:01.510
Yeah—

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:02.510
And profiting off of—

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:03.510
It actually shifted.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:04.510
That was the moment…

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:05.600
I’m not suggesting that you shouldn’t
pick up trash.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:10.560
But as far as I can tell, it was the first
moment where we shifted this responsibility

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.380
from the person selling to the person buying.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:15.790
That needs to change really quick.

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:18.220
And once it does, we won’t even have to
talk about

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:22.650
providing adequate recycling systems because
businesses will create products

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:27.210
that are easily and conveniently recyclable
because it will make more economic sense

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:32.560
for them if that burden is put on the business
instead of the consumer and the government.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:34.770
This gets to the heart of the matter.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.790
Climate change is a giant problem.

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:41.190
We’re not going to solve it without government
and industry taking action.

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:44.020
We live in this complicated web of carbon
emissions.

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:48.300
I mean, every single thing we do as individuals
creates pollution.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:49.540
It’s overwhelming.

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:55.180
But there’s one simple policy that could
make going green easier for all of us

00:05:55.180 --> 00:06:00.220
— and it could have an enormous impact:
We could put a price on carbon.

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:03.430
Right now, companies can emit as much pollution
as they like.

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:06.590
We’re basically treating our sky like a
giant sewer.

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:08.330
As long as it’s free to pollute,

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:10.530
no one’s going to stop doing it.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:16.000
You can't just go out there and find one source
or one factory, one business,

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.020
and shut it down and clean up your air.

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:21.580
Everybody in a sense is part of the problem.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:26.860
If companies had to pay for the carbon they
produce, it would encourage better behavior.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:30.370
This is what California did in 2006.

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:32.780
The state set a cap on carbon and

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:37.470
other greenhouse gas emissions and they lowered
it over the next few years.

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:42.090
Companies could either reduce their pollution
or pay for carbon allowances.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:44.030
And so far, it’s worked.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:46.750
The state is on track to hit its 2020 goal

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:51.180
— and they are looking to cut emissions
by another 40% by 2030.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:56.750
Now, California isn’t perfect, but this
is a huge reduction in emissions.

00:06:56.750 --> 00:07:00.630
It’s really larger than anything a person
could achieve on their own.

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:05.180
The fears that were raised by opponents have
not come to pass.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:12.150
We've not seen an exodus of industries from
California or people unable to drive their

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:13.150
cars.

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:18.490
And as the state cut emissions, California’s
economy has actually grown by 12%

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:20.420
— outpacing the national average.

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:24.170
Going green at this scale isn’t an overnight
process.

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:28.310
People like Mary Nichols have spent decades
fighting for better policies.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:32.990
We certainly have enjoyed a lot of political
support from all sides.

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:39.600
I think that's largely just because the public
in California has demanded that clean,

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:43.730
healthy air is something that everybody ought
to have access to.

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:48.870
So individual climate action does matter,
in the sense that it creates cultural change.

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:54.220
When Lauren makes a video tutorial or shares
one of her zero-waste tips on Instagram,

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:56.840
it has a social ripple effect.

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:59.560
Do you want everyone to live the lifestyle
you’re living?

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:02.060
I would never tell anyone how to live their
life.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:04.810
But I’d like to show everyone that there
are options.

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:09.400
That the way that we’re told we have to
live in this hyper-consumeristic way

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:15.400
isn’t the only way we have to live in order
to live in a modern world with modern luxuries.

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:21.090
Folks like Lauren really help build the bottom-up
support you need for large-scale transformation.

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:25.560
Look, climate policy can be complicated, and
sometimes it can be boring.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:28.120
But we need it to solve global warming.

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:34.010
And to get better policies like a price on
carbon, you need to have public support.

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:39.710
Because politicians and businesses won’t
take action unless people come together and

00:08:39.710 --> 00:08:41.940
demand it.

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:45.620
So you may not be able to fit all your trash
into a Mason jar.

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:50.620
But psychologists have been developing “green
nudges” that trick us into being more green.

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:53.630
Want to know whether they are working their
magic on you?

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:57.370
Visit

00:08:57.370 --> 00:08:57.940
to learn more.

